 "cards": [
  {
   "key": "whats_going_on",
   "title": "What's going on in the body",
   "text": "A paper reports that Niemann-Pick disease, type A is caused by changes in a gene called SMPD1, which carries the instructions for making a certain enzyme [E5967cc5, E7f24881]. According to HPO, this disease is also linked to the SMPD1 gene [E20201c7]. A paper reports that the missing enzyme is called acid sphingomyelinase, and it normally works inside a tiny part of the cell called the lysosome, which helps break down material the cell no longer needs [Eb4c571b]. A paper reports that because this enzyme does not work well, a fatty substance called sphingomyelin builds up inside cells instead of being broken down [E5f246b7]. According to Reactome, this enzyme is normally part of the body's system for breaking down fat-like molecules called glycosphingolipids [Eacfbed3, E41520eb]. According to HPO, Niemann-Pick A is passed down from parents to children in a pattern called autosomal recessive inheritance, meaning a child needs a non-working copy of the gene from each parent [E038bc7a]. According to HPO, some children with this condition have irritability [E667bc3a]. According to ClinVar, one specific change in the SMPD1 gene, called p.Arg389fs, has been confirmed as a cause of this disease [E85dc97c]. A paper reports that fat buildup inside brain cells can affect their outer coverings (membranes) and the connections between nerve cells [E4a3edc9].",
   "citations": [
    "E5967cc5",
    "E7f24881",
    "E20201c7",
